        "titel": "What you read and where it comes back",
        "tekst": "Session 2 of Professional Skills is called **Communicating adverse information**: how you pass on unwelcome news (a failed audit, an incident, a rejected proposal, a new restriction) without the message getting lost or the relationship getting damaged.\n\nThe lecture by Gohar Baghdasaryan covers four topics, and this page follows them:\n\n**1. Communication Accommodation Theory.** The reading on the slide is **Dragojevic, Gasiorek & Giles (2016)**, \"Accommodative strategies as core of the theory\", chapter 3 of the book *Communication Accommodation Theory* edited by Howard Giles (Cambridge University Press, pp. 36-59). The first Core material tab works through that chapter in its own order.\n\n**2. Communication channels, 3. \"The medium is the message\", 4. Communication barriers.** These three share the second Core material tab. The readings are **Ohiagu (2022)** on McLuhan, and the two barrier readings linked on the slide (a University of Waterloo teaching tip and an Indeed career guide).\n\n**Where it comes back.** Professional Skills has no knowledge exam. You are assessed through group presentations and an individual assignment, against competence 3, **Communication**: \"the student adapts appropriate communication styles for diverse audience, situations and objectives\". That sentence is CAT in one line. Adjusting to your audience is literally what you are graded on, so this session is the theory behind your presentation marks."
